---
description: >
  Apply when writing or reordering code within a single file: vertical grouping,
  line length, and composite literals whose field order matches the canonical
  type definition.
alwaysApply: true
---

# Vertical layout (within one file)

## VERT-01 — Separate concepts vertically

Different concepts must be separated by blank lines. A blank line is a visual cue that says "a new thought begins here." Group the import block, constant declarations, type definitions, and function bodies as distinct vertical sections.

```
// bad — all run together; reader must parse which part is which
import A
import B
LIMIT = 10
type User = { name: string }
function getUser() { ... }
function saveUser() { ... }

// good — concepts separated
import A
import B

LIMIT = 10

type User = { name: string }

function getUser() { ... }

function saveUser() { ... }
```

## VERT-02 — Keep related code vertically dense

Lines that are tightly related must appear together with no blank lines between them. Unnecessary blank lines within a logical unit force the reader to scroll to understand one thought.

```
// bad — blank lines interrupt a single unit of logic
result = fetchUser(id)

result.score = calculateScore(result)

return result

// good — one uninterrupted block
result = fetchUser(id)
result.score = calculateScore(result)
return result
```

## VERT-03 — Declare variables close to their usage

Declare a variable immediately before it is first used. Do not hoist all declarations to the top of a function that forces the reader to scroll back to find the type and origin.

```
// bad — declared far from use
name = ""
email = ""
role = ""
...30 lines later...
name = user.name
email = user.email
role = resolveRole(user)

// good — declared where needed
name = user.name
email = user.email
role = resolveRole(user)
sendWelcome(name, email, role)
```

## VERT-04 — Place dependent functions close together

When one function calls another, keep the callee immediately below or near the caller. Readers should be able to read down the file in the order they naturally encounter each function.

## VERT-05 — Keep lines short

Lines must be short enough to read without horizontal scrolling. A widely accepted limit is 120 characters. Prefer breaking at logical boundaries — after a comma, before an operator, or between chained calls.

## VERT-06 — Do not break indentation

Never misalign code to make it horizontally fit. Indentation encodes logical structure; breaking it destroys that signal.

```
// bad — closing brace misaligned; indentation no longer maps to nesting
if condition:
      doThing()
  doOtherThing()

// good — indentation consistently reflects nesting
if condition:
    doThing()
doOtherThing()
```

## VERT-07 — Composite literals follow the type declaration order

For any composite built from a named type (record, struct, typed object), list members in the **same order** as that type’s source declaration so type and literal read in parallel. Nested composites use **their** inner type’s order the same way.

```
// bad — literal lists second before first; reader cannot walk the type top to bottom
type Pair:
  field first
  field second
value pairLiteral:
  assign second = …
  assign first = …

// good — each assign follows the next field in the type
type Pair:
  field first
  field second
value pairLiteral:
  assign first = …
  assign second = …
```

```
// bad — nested block lists inner fields out of order vs Inner
type Inner:
  field timeout
  field mode
type Outer:
  field name
  field inner
value outerLiteral:
  assign name = …
  assign inner = nested:
    assign mode = …
    assign timeout = …

// good — outer follows Outer; nested follows Inner line for line
type Inner:
  field timeout
  field mode
type Outer:
  field name
  field inner
value outerLiteral:
  assign name = …
  assign inner = nested:
    assign timeout = …
    assign mode = …
```
